Subject:  Re: Carl's comments on my comments on the harassment policy
Date: 2 Jul 1993 18:55:04 -0400
Message-ID: <199307022255.AA03961@eff.org>
Carl Kadie writes WRT my posting yesterday:

>No where does the recommendation say that any "sexual" image
>constitutes harassment. I thought the recommended policy was clear
>that sexual harassment via computer is defined with the same
>definition and is handled with the same procedure as other types
>sexual harassment.
>
>In the recommended example, there isn't just a sexual image, there is
>a sexual image that makes it difficult for a student to work. Like it
>or not, that basically fits the definition of sexual harassment.  (I
>say "basically" because if her difficulty isn't reasonable, then it
>doesn't fit the legal definition.)
>
>So, there are 3 questions:
>
>1) Is the legal definition of sexual harassment among students too broad?
>2) Is the general U. of Illinois definition broader than the law allows?
>3) Is the recommended computer policy broader that the general university
>    definition or the legal definition?
>
>Questions 1 and 2 are interesting, but for the purpose of this policy,
>they are just assumed to be answered "no".
>
>Question 3 is the most important question here. If you think the
>answer is yes, please post.

Perhaps the recommended policy wasn't as clear as Carl thought it was, judging
from the amount of traffic in this and related threads.  Some reworking to
achieve the desired level of clarity may be in order.

WRT to the three questions, the answer to question 3 may indeed be "no;"
the proposed definition WRT computer use may not be more sweeping than the
legal or general university definition of harassment; but neither should it
be taken as a given, IMHO, that these policies are devoid of both overbreadth
and the separate, but related, defect of vagueness.

In the example contained within the policy and alluded to above, a woman reacts
negatively to images of "scantily clad females."  Potter Stewart's classic
comment on obscenity may be relevant here:  the material may need to be seen
in order to be determined beyond the pale or not.  These are difficult ques-
tions to resolve in the abstract.  How scantily clad is scantily clad, for
instance?  Is a classic Edward Weston nude photo to be construed identically
with a Hustler centerfold?

From a practical standpoint, more stress should be placed on "reasonableness"
as a limiting factor in allegations of harassment.  More stress should also
be placed on the difference between being harassed, and simply being offended;
to make the case for the former, it should be necessary to demonstrate that
some real untenability of the environment has resulted for the complainant
from the accused's display of a "sexual" image.

WRT the discussion of the recommended response by sysadmins, I think that in
practice, for admins to inform users that complaints have occurred seems
benign enough at face value; but in practice, that and the other recommended
response of recording the identities of the users in question for possible
later use could have the proverbial chilling effect on the user community,
even when complaints on which these admin actions are based turn out to be
unfounded.

                                           Mitch Pravatiner
